<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Korea under Japanese rule</h1><p class="st-meta">1910 CE – 1945 CE · capital Keijo (Seoul)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Korea's thirty-five years as a Japanese colony, a period of harsh rule, forced assimilation and uneven modernisation that still shapes relations in East Asia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After making the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/korean-empire">Korean Empire</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1905, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> formally annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> by treaty in August 1910. A Government-General headed by a governor-general, always a senior army or navy officer, was established at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Keijo</a>, the Japanese name for Seoul.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Early rule relied on military police; a land survey transferred much land to the colonial state and Japanese companies. The nationwide <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/march-first-movement">March First Movement</a> of 1919 was suppressed, but Governor-General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saito-makoto">Saito Makoto</a> then allowed limited press and associations. Railways, ports and northern heavy industry grew to serve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. From the late 1930s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> were forced to worship at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a> shrines and adopt Japanese names, and many were conscripted for labour, military service and sexual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan's</a> surrender on 15 August 1945 ended colonial rule. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> was divided at the 38th parallel between Soviet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/occupation-of-japan">American occupation</a>, leading to two Korean states in 1948.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/korea-under-japanese-rule.jpg" alt="Korea under Japanese rule" width="320" height="224"><figcaption>Image: kahusi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Japan_(1870%E2%80%931999).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1910 CE – 1945 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Keijo (Seoul)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Terauchi Masatake, Saito Makoto, Minami Jiro</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Confucianism, Christianity, Shinto</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Korean, Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a>, <a href="/countries/north-korea">North Korea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chosen, Government-General of Korea, Japanese Korea</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1928&amp;focus=korea-under-japanese-rule" aria-label="Korea under Japanese rule on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7852 826 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Korea under Japanese rule lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8172 1082L8173 1083M8172 1082L8529 1145M8172 1082L8173 1082M8172 1082L8173 1082M8172 1082L8115 1035"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8173 1083h0M8529 1145h0M8173 1082h0M8173 1082h0M8115 1035h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8172 1082h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8172 1082h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Keijo. <a href="/atlas?year=1928&amp;focus=korea-under-japanese-rule">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
